    "narrative": "LawDog\u2019s lineup for August 29th is anchored by **Bryce Harper** (78.57) and **Lawrence Butler** (74.61), who draw the most favorable matchups of the day against R. Johnson. With an opposing xERA of 5.16, this environment is ripe for production; **Bryce Harper** continues to provide high-floor elite value, while **Lawrence Butler** benefits from a massive 13% boost from his platoon dynamic. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (72.47) remains a core engine for the offense, maintaining his utility despite facing the lefty, Lynch IV. We are also leaning heavily into the matchup against Johnson with **Luis Arraez** (69.53) and the high-upside speed of **Justin Crawford** (63.67). While **Justin Crawford** carries an order penalty batting ninth, his ability to manufacture stolen bases\u2014the scarcest commodity in our pursuit\u2014makes him an essential, albeit lower-ceiling, play to address our roster\u2019s specific needs.\n\nStrategic depth remains a focus as we navigate a few marginal starts. **Jonathan Aranda** (63.09) secures the UTIL spot due to a 12% platoon edge against Buehler, edging out **Jung Hoo Lee** (54.79) for a starting role. **Jung Hoo Lee** was the most difficult bench decision today; despite a favorable matchup against Bratt, his negative platoon split against lefties ultimately moved him behind the efficiency of our starting outfield and infield cohort. Risks are present in the lineup as well, most notably with **Gleyber Torres** (55.46), who faces the sharp arm of Snell. While **Gleyber Torres** earns points for his leadoff positioning, the -15% SP skill adjustment makes him a volatile asset tonight. We are keeping a close eye on **Braden Montgomery** (55.64) and **Cal Raleigh** (58.00); both hover near the middle of our scoring distribution, requiring a clean game to justify their inclusion over the bench depth provided by **Daulton Varsho** or **Gavin Sheets**.",
